    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.SpeechNoProgram">No speech program found, so nothing can be spoken. Install speech-dispatcher (spd-say) or espeak-ng.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.SpeechTryFailed">The voice could not say that. Check that speech-dispatcher or espeak-ng works and that a sound device is connected.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.PushDpapi">The saved link comes from the Windows app, where it is encrypted for that Windows user. It can't be read here: paste it again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.PushKeptIn">The link is kept in a file only you can read: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.PushWriteFailed">The link could not be saved: its file could not be written.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.PushAwayNote">Away is read from the X server's idle time. Where the X server can't say, alerts are sent anyway.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.HotkeyClash">Same key as: {0}. Only one of them gets it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.HistOff">The alert history is switched off, so new alerts are not kept. Switch it on under Settings › Alerts › History.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.HistNoDialog">This desktop has no file dialog, so the file was saved in the settings folder.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.NotifyOn">Show desktop notifications</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.NotifyOn.Desc">Each alert you choose below shows a notification, with buttons to switch to that client or to snooze the alert for 15 minutes.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.EndFrozenRefused">MultiPreview could not end {0}'s client.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.ClosedNote">Client closed unexpectedly is not available on Linux: Linux does not tell MultiPreview how a client it did not start ended, so a crash looks the same as quitting.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.CommandsTitle">Commands from other programs</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.AcceptCommands.Desc">Lets a Stream Deck app, a desktop shortcut or a script tell the running MultiPreview to bring a character to the front, switch profile, or toggle thumbnails or focus mode. Only programs of your own account can.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.CommandsNote">Run MultiPreview again with a command, for example below; a Stream Deck app (StreamController, streamdeck-ui, OpenDeck) runs it from a button. eve-multipreview --help lists every command.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.UpdateNotWritable">MultiPreview's folder can't be written by your account. Download the new version from the release page.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.NotYet">Not available on Linux yet: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.VolumeDesc">Each client's volume on this desktop's sound server. A client remembers it for next time.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.ResizeAllDesc">Width × height. Apply to all resizes every thumbnail where it is, and new thumbnails start at this size.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.LookGallery.Desc">One click changes the settings window and its dialogs at once. The thumbnails stay as they are.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.LookHelp.Body">Each card shows a small preview of a look; one click picks it. The settings window and its dialogs change at once; nothing has to restart. The thumbnails, their menus and the desktop notifications stay as they are.&#x0A;&#x0A;**Classic** is the navy of the old settings window. **Windows 11** is the dark look of Windows 11's own Settings. **High contrast** is black and white, with larger text. The other nine have their own colours and type, and **Capsule**, **Blueprint**, **Ink** and **Terminal** also lay out the page their own way.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.LookThemeSection">Desktop light and dark</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.LookFollowTheme">Show Daylight while the desktop's style is light</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.LookFollowTheme.Desc">Dark, no preference, or a desktop that does not say shows the look you picked. The look you pick stays saved.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.LookThemeOn">The desktop's style is light, so Daylight is shown. The look you pick is kept and shows when the desktop goes dark, or now if you turn the switch off.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.LookThemeHelp.Title">Desktop light and dark</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.LookThemeHelp.Body">With **Show Daylight while the desktop's style is light** on, MultiPreview shows **Daylight** whenever your desktop asks apps for a light style (the light or dark setting of GNOME, KDE Plasma and other desktops that tell apps through the freedesktop settings portal), and the look you picked when it is dark. A change on the desktop shows at once, without a restart.&#x0A;&#x0A;Your pick is never overwritten: it stays saved and shows again when the desktop goes dark or when you turn the switch off. If the desktop does not say, your own look shows. The switch is off by default and changes the settings window only, not the thumbnails.</sys:String>
    <!-- S5: Linux help wording -->
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.HubIntro">On Linux, alerts show as your desktop's notifications. Each names the client and the event, with buttons to switch to that client or to snooze the alert.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.Hub">Which severities show a notification is set under Look &amp; severity. Click a notification, or its Switch button, to go to that client; Snooze silences that alert for that character for 15 minutes. Where notifications appear and how they stack is up to your desktop. The buttons need notify-send 0.7.9 or later.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.CharacterClients">- **Never minimize** keeps this client open when the others are minimized on a switch.&#x0A;- **Game volume** sets this client's volume in the desktop's sound server (PulseAudio or PipeWire). It's remembered for next time.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.ClientState">A frozen or disconnected client looks like an idle one until you switch to it. MultiPreview can mark its thumbnail:&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Not responding**: the client's window has not answered the window manager for five seconds. The mark goes when it answers again, and the thumbnail's menu can end the client; MultiPreview asks first.&#x0A;- **Disconnected**: a logged-in client has had no connection to EVE's server for the time you set. MultiPreview reads the client's connections on this PC; it sends nothing anywhere.&#x0A;&#x0A;A client whose connections go through a proxy on this PC is never marked Disconnected. Both marks are off until you turn them on.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.Speech">With **Speak alerts** on, a voice says whose alert it is and what happened, in MultiPreview's language, through speech-dispatcher (spd-say) or espeak-ng.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Speak which alerts** chooses by severity.&#x0A;- Alerts of one event that arrive together are one sentence. An alert that waited more than 10 seconds is not said.&#x0A;- Do not disturb holds the voice as it holds the sounds. While **Critical alerts still play** is on, critical alerts are still spoken.&#x0A;- **Voices** sets a voice for each severity. Automatic takes a voice of MultiPreview's language when espeak-ng has one. **Try** says an example.&#x0A;- If the voice fails three times in a row, MultiPreview stops asking it until **Speak alerts** is switched off and on again, or until **Try** is heard.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.KeepAwake">A mining or industry session ends when the PC goes to sleep because nobody touched the mouse. With **Keep the PC awake while EVE runs** on, MultiPreview asks systemd (systemd-inhibit) not to sleep while at least one client has a character logged in.&#x0A;&#x0A;- The screen still turns off on its own timer.&#x0A;- When the last client closes, when you turn this off, or when MultiPreview closes, the PC may sleep again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.ClientStops">**Client not responding** is raised when a client's window has not answered for 30 seconds, once for each freeze. It needs a character logged in on that client. It is a critical alert, so do not disturb lets its sound through unless you changed that.&#x0A;&#x0A;**Client closed unexpectedly** is not available on Linux: Linux does not tell MultiPreview how a client it did not start ended, so a crash looks the same as quitting.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.Language">**Automatic** uses your desktop's language when MultiPreview has it, and English otherwise.&#x0A;&#x0A;Translations other than English were drafted by machine. If something reads wrong, say so on GitHub and it gets fixed in the next release.&#x0A;&#x0A;**Settings window size** only changes this window, not the thumbnails.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.GeneralIntro">Choose the language, how big this window is, and what MultiPreview does when it starts.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.Startup">**When MultiPreview starts** decides whether this window opens too. Choose minimized to have it ready without covering EVE.&#x0A;&#x0A;To start MultiPreview when you sign in, add it to your desktop's autostart programs.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.Severity">Every event is **critical**, **warning** or **info**. Each severity has a colour, a flash speed and a choice of whether its alerts show a desktop notification. Info alerts don't blink: they stay steady.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.Clicks">A plain click switches to that client. **Ctrl+click** (either Ctrl key) and **Shift+click** can minimize it or skip it when cycling instead. Ctrl is also EVE's lock-target key, so pick what suits how you play.&#x0A;&#x0A;**Use per-character colours** turns on the border and text colours you set on each character's page.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Linux.Help.Position">MultiPreview can move each client when it's found and whenever you switch to it:&#x0A;- **Centred on the monitor**&#x0A;- **At a fixed X and Y**&#x0A;&#x0A;**Remember where each client window was** puts every client back where you left it instead.</sys:String>

</ResourceDictionary>
